    "body": "Osaka, Japan-headquartered Kansai Electric Power is acquiring a minority interest in Prospect Power Storage, a 150-MW/600-MWh battery energy storage system (BESS) owned by Elevate Infrastructure and ArcLight Capital Partners .\nElevate (a portfolio company of ArcLight) and ArcLight announced the deal on November 16. Prospect Power currently is the largest standalone BESS in commercial operation in the PJM Interconnection, the power grid operator that serves all or part of 13 states across the U.S. Mid-Atlantic and Midwest regions. Prospect Power is located in Rockingham County, Virginia.\nThe companies on Monday said the investment by Kansai is part of Elevate’s strategy “to develop and operate critical energy infrastructure at scale while attracting sophisticated long-term strategic capital.” The groups said the BESS is an important installation in an area with growing electricity demand driven by data centers, artificial intelligence, and electrification.\nUtility-Scale Battery Energy Storage\n“Prospect Power demonstrates Elevate’s ability to execute utility-scale battery storage projects from acquisition through commercial operation and deliver flexible capacity where the grid needs it most,” said Joshua Rogol, CEO of Elevate. “As electricity demand accelerates, projects like Prospect Power are increasingly important to strengthening grid reliability and supporting economic growth. We look forward to continuing to bring projects of this scale online across our portfolio.”\nThe groups said that Kansai’s investment “further expands the group of global strategic and institutional investors partnering with Elevate and ArcLight and reinforces the ability of the platform to combine deep energy infrastructure expertise, operational execution, and long-term capital.”\n“Battery storage is becoming increasingly critical as energy demand grows and power markets require greater flexibility and reliability,” said Angelo Acconcia, managing partner of ArcLight. “Prospect Power demonstrates the value of bringing large-scale storage infrastructure into service in high-growth markets, and Kansai’s investment is a strong validation of both the quality of the asset and the growing need for storage as part of the nation’s power infrastructure.”\n“Prospect Power is a high-quality operating asset in an important U.S. power market, and we are pleased to partner with Elevate and ArcLight through this investment – our first battery storage project outside Japan,” said Takayuki Doi, executive officer of Kansai’s Global Business. “We look forward to leveraging the experience gained through Prospect Power and exploring opportunities for further collaboration in battery storage and other energy infrastructure in the United States.”\nElevate brought Prospect Power into commercial operation in May of this year . The project is fully contracted under a 15-year power purchase agreement with Dominion Energy Virginia.\n— Darrell Proctor is a senior editor for POWER.\nTagged in:\nArcLight\nElevate\nEnergy storage\nJapan\nKansai\nKEPCO\nVirginia\nSept. 28—30, 2026\nWashington, D.C.\nRegister Now\nExplore this topic and more — live at Experience POWER. The power industry's most urgent decisions are being made right now. Be in the room where they happen.\nView Conference Program",
